## The three problems

### `parser` · the shared parser reads day-first dates month first · fixed

Every tenant on file writes dates day first. The shared parser read every date month first, so
the day and the month swapped: "Booked for 2 December. The invite says 12 February." It came in
by app, by email, and through booking-v2 forms, whose hint asks for `DD/MM/YYYY`. This is the bug
#4471's title names. Fixed by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The fix names EU locales;
`harbour.dental` (`en-GB`) is day-first too, so check its next invites: day-first is a question
for the locale, not for a list of countries.

### `hint` · booking-v3 asks for the month first · won't fix

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants on booking-v3
typed the month first, as the hint told them, and the form read the date the tenant's way, day
first. Same swap, so subject-line triage merged it into #4471, but the parser fix does not touch
it. Won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired. Reports stop when its last tenants move off it.

### `one-day` · a date moves by one day · open

Some reports are not a swap at all. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May."
"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." "The invite arrived
before I made the booking." A day/month swap never moves a date by exactly one day, so neither the
parser fix nor retiring booking-v3 closes these. A different day for people in different time
zones, and one tenant who sees it "only in June", point at a date kept as midnight and converted
through another time zone or a daylight-saving offset. Nobody owns it yet. It needs its own
investigation.

## How the reports were counted

- **One id, one report.** The ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so some reports appear
  twice. Each id is counted once.
- **The tenant decides, not the intake tag.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser.
  The cause depends on the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`, `locales.ts`).
- **Counted as the desk counts.** Every report is counted once, where its tenant files it,
  exactly as the #4471 table counts it. The table checks that sum against the ticket.
- **A one-day move is its own problem.** The desk already read these apart (`desk-notes.json`).
  They stay counted where their tenant files them, as the #4471 footnote says, and they also
  get their own row and their own ticket. Each report body was checked: every other report
  names a swapped day and month, or is consistent with one.
- The numbers come from the data at render time (`problems.ts`, `ProblemTable.svelte`), so they
  stay right when the desk merges more reports.

## What this changes for the desk

- #4471 was not one known issue, and closing it did not close the one-day move. Triage new date
  reports to the ticket that matches their cause.
- The subject line cannot tell these problems apart: the same subjects ("Wrong day on the
  invite", "Date is wrong", "Month and day swapped?") come in for all three. Subject-line dedupe
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) planned for one bug under different
  subjects; here it is different bugs under the same subjects. Splitting needs the tenant and
  the report body. That needs a new decision record; 0001 stays as it is.

